---
import AppWindow from '~/components/ui/AppWindow.astro';
import PageHeader from '~/components/ui/PageHeader.astro';
import ThemedImage from '~/components/ui/ThemedImage.astro';
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { REVOLUT_PATH_TEXT, STATEMENT_CHOICES, STATEMENT_TRANSFER_TIP } from '~/data/statement';
import { ISSUES_URL } from '~/navigation';
import { shot } from '~/utils/screenshots';

const [format, range] = STATEMENT_CHOICES.map(({ value }) => value);

const sections = [
  { id: 'statement', title: 'Get your statement' },
  { id: 'first-time', title: 'The first time' },
  { id: 'every-time', title: 'Every time after that' },
  { id: 'no-new-statement', title: 'No new statement needed' },
  { id: 'confirm', title: 'Which listing is it?' },
  { id: 'read-home', title: 'Read Home' },
  { id: 'missing-price', title: 'When a price is missing' },
  { id: 'spreadsheet', title: 'Your Excel spreadsheet' },
  { id: 'google-sheets', title: 'Using Google Sheets' },
  { id: 'settings', title: 'Settings' },
  { id: 'privacy', title: 'Privacy' },
  { id: 'limitations', title: 'Known limitations' },
  { id: 'problem', title: 'Report a problem' },
];

const metadata = {
  title: 'Guide · Statement Portfolio Tracker',
  description:
    'How to get your Revolut statement, set the app up, read Home and keep your Excel spreadsheet up to date, even by itself.',
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 md:px-6 pt-14 md:pt-20 pb-24 md:pb-32">
    <PageHeader title="Guide">More details on how the app works.</PageHeader>

    <div class="mt-12 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-[14rem_1fr] lg:gap-16">
      <nav aria-label="On this page" class="hidden lg:block">
        <ul class="sticky top-24 space-y-1 text-sm border-l border-gray-200 dark:border-white/10">
          {
            sections.map(({ id, title }) => (
              <li>
                <a
                  href={`#${id}`}
                  data-toc={id}
                  class="-ml-px block border-l border-transparent pl-4 py-1 text-muted hover:text-default hover:border-gray-400 data-[active]:text-primary data-[active]:border-primary"
                >
                  {title}
                </a>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </nav>

      <article
        class="prose prose-lg dark:prose-invert max-w-3xl prose-headings:tracking-tight prose-headings:scroll-mt-24 prose-a:text-primary prose-table:text-base prose-th:text-left"
      >
        <h2 id="statement">Get your statement</h2>
        <p>In the Revolut app:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Tap <b>{REVOLUT_PATH_TEXT}</b>.</li>
          <li>Choose <b>{format}</b> as the format and <b>{range}</b> as the date range.</li>
          <li>{STATEMENT_TRANSFER_TIP}</li>
        </ol>
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            <b>Always choose All time.</b> The app needs your whole history, from your first trade until today, to know what
            you still hold. A shorter range lists only the buys and sells inside it, so the results would be wrong or the
            file would be rejected as incomplete.
          </p>
        </blockquote>

        <h2 id="first-time">The first time</h2>
        <p>The app opens on a short welcome in two steps.</p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <b>Your statement.</b> Drop the CSV file from Revolut on the window, or click <b>Choose statement file…</b> The
            app reads it straight away and shows what it found: how many transactions, the date of the latest one, and how
            many investments you still hold. If the file isn’t right (another range than All time, say), it explains what
            is wrong. Click <b>Continue</b>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Keep a spreadsheet up to date?</b> Choose <b>Use a spreadsheet I have</b> to pick an Excel file you already
            have, <b>Create a new one</b> to let the app make one where you choose, or <b>Not now</b> to only see the values
            in the app. Click <b>Show my investments</b>.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h2 id="every-time">Every time after that</h2>
        <p>
          The app opens on <b>Home</b> and looks up today’s prices by itself: the amounts fill in after a few seconds. Home
          has three parts:
        </p>
        <div class="not-prose my-8">
          <AppWindow
            shot={shot('home')}
            alt="The app’s Home: the totals, your statement and your spreadsheet, and your investments"
          />
        </div>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <b>The totals:</b> what everything is worth now, your gain or loss since you bought, and what you invested. <b
              >Refresh prices</b
            > looks the prices up again.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Your statement</b> and <b>your spreadsheet</b>, side by side: where the values come from, and where they
            go.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Your investments:</b> one row each, and the total. You can sort them, show only some, and choose the columns.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          To update your Excel file, press <b>Update my spreadsheet</b>. The app writes the values shown and says what
          it wrote, in green, or in red with what to do if it couldn’t save the file (usually because it is open in
          Excel: close it there and press <b>Try again</b>). <b>Open folder</b> shows the file in its folder. To switch to
          another spreadsheet, press <b>Use another…</b>, or drop an Excel file on the window. If the spreadsheet has
          been moved or deleted, Home says so.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nothing is written until you press the button, unless you turn on <b>Update it by itself</b> in
          <a href="#settings">Settings</a>: the app then writes your spreadsheet each time it has worked out today’s
          values. <b>Help</b> (the <b>?</b> at the top) shows where to get the statement, how your data is handled and the
          app’s version.
        </p>

        <h2 id="no-new-statement">No new statement needed</h2>
        <p>
          The app keeps a copy of the last statement you used, so it doesn’t need a new one each time. <b
            >Your statement</b
          > on Home names the file it was copied from and says the date of its latest transaction. The copy still works if
          you move or delete that file.
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            <b>Bought, sold or added money since then?</b> The statement from last time doesn’t know about it. Get a new
            one from Revolut and drop it anywhere on the window, or press <b>Use a newer statement…</b> The app keeps that
            one from then on.
          </p>
        </blockquote>

        <h2 id="confirm">Which listing is it?</h2>
        <p>
          Many funds are traded on several stock exchanges under the same code. When the app can’t tell which one is
          yours, it asks once, listing them with their exchange, currency and symbol, and marks those that match what
          you paid. They are usually the same fund at almost the same price. You can:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <b>Use this listing:</b> pick the one whose name and currency match what you see in Revolut. If yours isn’t there,
            type its Yahoo Finance symbol in the box under the list.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>From now on, take the first listing without asking:</b> the listing whose past prices best match what you
            paid always comes first, which is usually right.
          </li>
          <li><b>Skip this investment:</b> it is left without a current value.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The app remembers your answer. To change it later, use <a href="#settings">Settings</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="read-home">Read Home</h2>
        <p>
          <b>Worth now</b> is what everything you hold would be worth at today’s prices, in your statement’s currency. Under
          it, your gain or loss since you bought, with an arrow, a sign and a colour. <b>Invested</b> is what you paid for
          what you still hold, fees included.
        </p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Investment</td><td>The fund or share: its name, with its code and stock exchange under it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Invested</td><td>What you paid for what you still hold, fees included.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Worth now</td><td>What it would be worth at today’s price. Approximate.</td></tr>
            <tr
              ><td>Share of total</td><td>How much of all your investments this one is, by what they are worth now.</td
              ></tr
            >
            <tr><td>Gain or loss</td><td>Worth now minus invested, and as a percentage.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
          The last row is the total. Investments you have sold completely are not listed. <b>Columns</b> adds others: shares,
          average cost per share, price per share, share of invested, gain from sales and dividends received.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <b>Sort:</b> click a column’s heading, the largest amounts first; click again for the other way round, and a
            third time for the usual order.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Show some:</b>
            <b>Show</b> chooses between all investments, those gaining and those losing; <b>Search by name or ticker</b>
            finds one. The total then covers only those shown, and a line says so, with <b>Show all</b>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Rename:</b> double-click an investment’s name (or click it and press <kbd>F2</kbd>) and type the name you
            want; it is used in your spreadsheet too.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Undo:</b>
            <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> takes back the last change to an investment, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Y</kbd> makes
            it again.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>The app remembers your columns, the order and what Show is set to.</p>

        <h2 id="missing-price">When a price is missing</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <b>“No price today”</b>: the app couldn’t get a current price. Its row is grey, and the totals cover the
            other investments only: the app never guesses a value.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>“price from” a date</b>: Yahoo Finance couldn’t be reached, so the last price found on an earlier day is
            used. The totals say so, with <b>Try again</b>.
          </li>
          <li><b>“your value”</b>: one you entered yourself.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <b>Enter its value…</b> in the investment’s row (or a double-click on the row) lets you type what the whole investment
          is worth now, as the Revolut app shows it. You can also type it over “unavailable” in your spreadsheet. The app
          keeps your value until Yahoo Finance has a price again.
        </p>

        <h2 id="spreadsheet">Your Excel spreadsheet</h2>
        <p>
          Each update writes two sheets of your file, creating them the first time. Close the file in Excel first: the
          app can’t save a file that Excel has open.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <b>Current Holdings</b>: one row per investment you hold, and a Total. Percentages and the Total are Excel
            formulas, so they follow if you change a value. The whole sheet is rewritten each time, so keep your notes
            elsewhere.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Portfolio History</b>: one row per day you use the app (a second update on the same day changes that
            row), with what you put in, what it is worth, unrealized and realized gains, ROI %, net worth, net deposits
            and gain vs deposits.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <b>New or existing?</b>
          <b>Create a new one</b> makes a file with only the app’s two sheets: the safest choice. With a file you have, your
          own sheets stay as they are: values, formulas, formatting, colour rules, tables and charts are kept, and can read
          from the app’s sheets. <b>Pictures, shapes and text boxes are lost</b>, because the app saves the whole file
          again; if your file has any, create a new spreadsheet instead.
        </p>
        <p>
          <b>Backups:</b> the first time each day the app changes an existing file, it saves a copy next to it, named with
          the date, for example <code>My investments_backup_2026-10-15.xlsx</code>. One is kept; you can keep more in
          Settings. To go back, open a copy and save it under your spreadsheet’s name.
        </p>

        <h2 id="google-sheets">Using Google Sheets</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>
            Install <a href="https://www.google.com/drive/download/">Google Drive for desktop</a> and sign in. Your Drive
            appears on your computer like a folder.
          </li>
          <li>
            When the app asks for a spreadsheet, create it inside your Drive, or choose an Excel file already there.
          </li>
          <li>
            Open it from drive.google.com. Google Sheets edits it as an Excel file (it shows a small .XLSX label).
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          After each update, reload the page to see the new values. Close the file in Google Sheets before the app
          updates it. <b>File › Save as Google Sheets</b> makes a separate copy, which the app does not update.
        </p>

        <h2 id="settings">Settings</h2>
        <p>Press <b>Settings</b> at the top of Home. Changes are saved at once.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <b>Your investments:</b> each one’s <b>⋯</b> button offers <b>Rename</b>, <b>Choose the listing next time</b
            >
            and <b>Forget…</b> And the switch <b>Take the first listing without asking</b>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Your statement:</b> the one the app keeps, with <b>Use a newer one…</b> and <b>Forget it…</b>
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Your spreadsheet:</b>
            <b>Choose another…</b>, <b>Stop using it</b>, how many backup copies to keep, and <b>Update it by itself</b
            >: the app writes your spreadsheet each time today’s prices are in, without the button.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Opening the app:</b>
            <b>Open when I sign in</b> opens the app each time you sign in to your computer, minimized in the taskbar (the
            Dock on a Mac), where it works out today’s values. With Update it by itself on, your spreadsheet is then updated
            every day without you opening anything. When the app needs you, its taskbar button flashes. Only the installed
            app can do this; on a Mac, move it to Applications first.
          </li>
          <li><b>Appearance:</b> light, dark, or as your computer is set.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="not-prose my-8 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-white/10 shadow-xl">
          <ThemedImage
            shot={shot('settings-opening')}
            alt="Settings, with “Update it by itself” and “Open when I sign in” turned on"
            sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 48rem, 100vw"
          />
        </div>
        <p>
          Opening the app while it is already open, minimized or not, brings up the window that is open instead of a
          second one.
        </p>

        <h2 id="privacy">Privacy</h2>
        <p>
          Everything happens on your computer. There are no accounts, and the app doesn’t record how you use it. The
          only thing that leaves your computer is the codes of your investments and dates, sent to Yahoo Finance to look
          up prices. Your statement and your amounts are never sent anywhere, and none of the amounts in your statement
          are kept by the app.
        </p>
        <p>
          The app keeps a few kilobytes in your user folder: its settings (your confirmed investments, where your
          spreadsheet is), the last prices found, values you entered, and a copy of the last statement you used, so that
          it needs no new one each time. That copy holds your whole trading history, like the file you downloaded from
          Revolut; <a href="#settings">Settings</a> can forget it. Uninstalling the app on Windows deletes all of it, and
          takes the app out of your startup apps.
        </p>

        <h2 id="limitations">Known limitations</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Only Revolut’s stock statement in CSV format, with the “All time” range, is supported.</li>
          <li>The statement must be in a single currency.</li>
          <li>
            Only cash top-ups, market buys, market sells and dividends are understood. Anything else (a stock split, a
            withdrawal) stops the app with a message naming the row, so it never shows numbers built on a guess.
          </li>
          <li>
            Prices need an internet connection. Without one, the app uses the last prices it found, dated, or shows “No
            price today”; you can then <a href="#missing-price">enter values yourself</a>.
          </li>
          <li>The figures are simple: what you paid against what it is worth now. No tax calculations.</li>
          <li>
            English only. The app doesn’t update itself: new versions are on the <a href="/download">download page</a>.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="problem">Report a problem</h2>
        <p>
          Open an issue on <a href={ISSUES_URL}>GitHub</a>. If the app showed an error box, click <b>Copy details</b> there
          and paste the text into the issue. <b>Don’t attach your real statement</b>: it contains your trades and
          amounts. Describe the problem and the row the message mentions instead.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<script>
  // Highlight the section being read in the contents.
  const links = new Map(
    Array.from(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-toc]')).map((a) => [a.dataset.toc!, a])
  );
  const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
    (entries) => {
      entries.forEach((entry) => {
        if (!entry.isIntersecting) return;
        links.forEach((a) => a.removeAttribute('data-active'));
        links.get(entry.target.id)?.setAttribute('data-active', '');
      });
    },
    { rootMargin: '0px 0px -70% 0px' }
  );
  links.forEach((_, id) => {
    const heading = document.getElementById(id);
    if (heading) observer.observe(heading);
  });
</script>
